<h2>The seizure and the stalled trial</h2>
<p>The case began with a roadside interception in July 2023: 432.7 kg of ganja — a commercial quantity many times over — concealed behind Poclain machines on a truck the petitioner was driving. He and his helper were arrested on the spot; the alleged financiers and procurers were arrested later and are on bail. More than three years on, charges had not been framed. The Special Court attributed the delay to the absence of bailed co-accused, a change of Presiding Officer and pending non-bailable warrants. It was against that backdrop that the driver sought regular bail under Section 483 of the BNSS.</p>

<h2>Three arguments, three answers</h2>
<p>The petition rested on three planks: that nothing was recovered from the petitioner personally; that the grounds of arrest were never communicated as Section 47 BNSS and Article 22(1) of the Constitution require; and that three years of custody without charges being framed had itself become unconstitutional detention.</p>

<p>The first argument collapsed on the record — the petitioner's own pleadings described the ganja as seized from him while transporting it, and the Court observed that the plea appeared to have been copied from the petitions of other accused. The reminder for drafting counsel is blunt: a bail petition recycled from a co-accused's file can destroy an otherwise arguable case.</p>

<h2>Adequacy versus absence of grounds of arrest</h2>
<p>The second argument engaged the fast-developing jurisprudence on grounds of arrest. The Court's framework ran as follows. The requirement of written grounds laid down in <em>Mihir Rajesh Shah</em> applies prospectively from 6 November 2025, following <em>State of Meghalaya v. Sonam Raghuvanshi</em>, so it did not govern a July 2023 arrest. For arrests before that date, <em>Vihaan Kumar</em> governs: the grounds must be effectively communicated in a language the arrestee understands, and the burden of proving compliance lies on the arresting officer. Applying that test, the Court held the petitioner's real complaint was about the adequacy of what the arrest memo recorded, not the absence of communication — and, quoting the Supreme Court, non-service of grounds of arrest and non-furnishing of adequate reasons are different things. A man arrested at the spot with the contraband, who twice sought bail without ever raising the plea, could not show the prejudice that an adequacy challenge requires.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The distinction matters across statutes. Total non-communication of grounds of arrest vitiates the arrest itself and can found release notwithstanding restrictive bail provisions. A complaint that the grounds were thin, formulaic or insufficiently detailed is a different and weaker challenge — it requires demonstrated prejudice, and conduct such as earlier bail petitions silent on the point will be held against the accused.</p>
</div>

<h2>Section 37 and the answer to delay</h2>
<p>On the third plank, the Court declined to treat prolonged incarceration as a trump over Section 37 of the NDPS Act: in a commercial-quantity case the right to a speedy trial has to be read alongside, and not in displacement of, the twin conditions. But the order did not leave the undertrial where it found him. In a sentence that deserves quotation — the answer to a trial that has not moved is to move the trial — the Court directed the Special Court to frame charges within four weeks, to refuse to let the absence of bailed co-accused stall the trial of those in custody, to examine witnesses at intervals of no more than fourteen days, and to endeavour to conclude the trial within six months, with liberty to the petitioner to move afresh if that timetable fails for reasons not attributable to him.</p>

<h2>For the practitioner</h2>
<p>The order maps the realistic strategy in commercial-quantity NDPS matters. A grounds-of-arrest challenge must be pleaded early, precisely, and with attention to the date of arrest, because the written-grounds mandate is prospective and the adequacy route demands proof of prejudice. And where Section 37 blocks conventional bail, time-bound trial directions with liberty to renew are often the most valuable relief actually on offer — they convert the speedy-trial right from rhetoric into a calendar, and they define in advance the changed circumstance that will sustain the next application.</p>
